# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Security headers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is a static site with no accounts and no session cookies, so the surface
# is small — but the two intake forms POST to a Pages Function, and the site is
# the public face of a project whose whole argument is that it can be trusted.
#
# **The `Content-Security-Policy` line below is a placeholder.** It is rewritten
# after every build by `scripts/generate-csp.mjs`, which hashes every inline
# script the site actually shipped and writes them into `script-src`. Editing it
# here has no effect on what deploys; edit the generator.
#
# It is generated because Astro emits inline bootstraps for islands, and their
# contents change whenever the island does. A hand-maintained hash list would be
# correct on the day it was written and silently wrong afterwards — and the
# usual repair for "silently wrong" is restoring `unsafe-inline`, which is the
# one setting that turns a CSP off. `csp:check` fails a build whose committed
# policy no longer matches its output.
#
# `style-src` keeps `unsafe-inline` deliberately: Astro emits scoped component
# CSS inline, and no injection path this policy leaves open runs through a
# stylesheet. Recorded in docs/SECURITY.md rather than left unexplained.
#
# The only third-party origin allowed anywhere in this policy is
# `static.cloudflareinsights.com`, which serves the analytics beacon. There is
# no analytics `connect-src` entry: the beacon posts to Cloudflare's own edge on
# the same origin as the site, which is one of the reasons it was chosen.
#
# `connect-src` is `'self'` and nothing else. The validator, the badge, the
# registry status endpoint and the contact form are all same-origin; a site that
# needed a third-party connect entry to work would be a site with a dependency
# its privacy note did not mention.
#
# There is no `frame-src` and no `frame-ancestors 'self'`: nothing on this site
# embeds a frame, and nothing should embed this site.

/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com https://cloud.umami.is; connect-src 'self' https://cloud.umami.is; upgrade-insecure-requests
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), interest-cohort=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload

# The agent-readiness surface.
#
# What the site publishes for agents is the `.md` twin of every page, and
# `llms.txt`. That is the whole list, and it is deliberately short.
#
# Removed in the launch dry run: the OAuth metadata endpoints, `auth.md`, the
# agent-skills index, an `api-catalog` link header pointing at a file that was
# never generated, and `Link` headers on an `/en/` route that does not exist
# under D-W1. All were artifacts describing an authorization surface
# this site does not have — `auth.md` documented `/agent/register`,
# `/agent/claim` and `/oauth/revoke`, none of them implemented here.
#
# A published discovery document for endpoints that do not exist is precisely
# the failure this project measures other people's manifests for.
#
# Restored since, because these three describe endpoints that do exist:
# `/openapi.json` (the three public, authless endpoints, and nothing else),
# `/.well-known/api-catalog` (RFC 9727, pointing at that document and at the
# human pages beside it), and the `Link` headers below (RFC 8288).
#
# Still absent, and staying absent: OAuth and OIDC discovery, the protected
# resource metadata, `auth.md` and an MCP server card. This site has no
# authorization server, no protected resource and no deployed MCP endpoint,
# and a card advertising one would be the same lie in a newer format.

# Agent discovery, on every HTML page. `api-catalog` and `service-desc` are
# IANA-registered relations; `describedby` points an agent at the file written
# for it rather than at the rendered page.
/*
  Link: </.well-known/api-catalog>; rel="api-catalog"; type="application/linkset+json", </openapi.json>; rel="service-desc"; type="application/openapi+json", </llms.txt>; rel="describedby"; type="text/plain"

# RFC 9727 says the catalog is `application/linkset+json`. It has no file
# extension, so nothing infers the type for it.
/.well-known/api-catalog
  Content-Type: application/linkset+json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/openapi.json
  Content-Type: application/openapi+json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

# Agent-markdown twins. `text/markdown` is deliberate, and browsers have no
# renderer for that type — they download the file or show a blank viewport.
# `Content-Disposition: inline` tells them to display it instead, without
# changing what an agent fetches.
/*.md
  Content-Disposition: inline
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/images/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/icons/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/favicon.ico
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/favicon.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/site.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, must-revalidate
